==Icelandic==
{{cardinalbox|is|3|4|5|þrír|fimm|ord=fjórði}}

===Etymology===
{{dercat|is|ine-pro|inh=1}}
From {{inh|is|non|fjórir}}, from {{inh|is|gem-pro|*fedwōr}}.<ref>{{R:is:IOSB|page=181}}</ref> Cognates include {{cog|fo|fýra}} and {{cog|da|fire}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|is|/ˈfjouːrɪr/}}

===Numeral===
{{head|is|numeral|cat2=cardinal numbers|feminine|fjórar|neuter|fjögur}}

# [[four]]
#: {{uxi|is|Þar sátu '''fjórir''' menn.|'''Four''' men were sitting there.}}
#: {{uxi|is|Hún er '''fjögurra'''.|She is four years old.}}

====Usage notes====
* When counting out loud, the contraction {{m|is|fjór}} is frequently used.
*: {{uxi|is|Einn, tveir, þrír, '''fjór'''.|One, two, three, '''four'''.}}

====Declension====
{{is-decl-fjórir}}

The genitive {{m|is||fjögra}} is common in speech, but {{m|is||fjögurra}} is preferred in writing.
A recent alternative genitive form, {{m|is|fjagra}}, is very widespread in spoken language, but is only occasionally used in writing.

====Derived terms====
* {{l|is|fjarki}}
* {{l|is|klukkan fjögur}}
* {{l|is|undir fjögur augu}}
* {{l|is|á fjórum fótum}}

